==English==

===Etymology===
Variant of {{m|en|thole}}, from {{inh|en|enm|tholen}}, {{m|enm|tholien}}, from {{inh|en|ang|þolian||to bear; endure}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|thoil}}. More at {{l|en|thole}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|en|ɔɪl|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|Yorkshire|transitive}} To be [[able]] to [[justify]] the [[expense]] of.
#: {{ux|en|Aw lov'd them red shoon but Aw coun't '''thoil''' em in addition to t'new dress Aw'd bowt.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1996|title=Transactions of the Yorkshire Dialect Society
|passage=But yon poor widder-woman, strugglin' along on a bit of a pension, 'ad nowt left but two coppers - but sh' '''thoiled''' it, an' put it in, all t' same!}}

